prompt:
Below are 5 choices made by one researcher. In each, the researcher was shown two positions (a and b) at a research decision point and held the one marked "chosen".

For EACH choice independently, propose at most 2 preference principles that explain why this researcher chose that option over the other. Each principle is one concise sentence of at most 600 characters, phrased as a general preference ("Prefers X over Y because Z").

Return only JSON: {"C1": ["principle", ...], "C2": [...], "C3": [...], "C4": [...], "C5": [...]}

CHOICES:
{
 "C1": {
  "context": "At decision point D3, which position does this researcher hold?",
  "a": "Uses a skeptical second-line stress-testing function that challenges load-bearing safety claims, while staying grounded in day-to-day work without becoming contributors who compromise independence.",
  "b": "Distrusts behavior measured on a single training prompt, since RL can install non-robust heuristics that vanish under small prompt changes (e.g., swapping [ORG] for [ORG]), so robustness across prompt variations is required for a convincing result.",
  "chosen": "a"
 },
 "C2": {
  "context": "At decision point D6, which position does this researcher hold?",
  "a": "prefers disclosing the full research process (first environments built, no unreported ones, runs counted, a bug found) over presenting only clean results, because it lets readers judge selection effects.",
  "b": "Prefers modest, scoped claims (proof of viability, simple baseline) over claiming the technique suffices for high-stakes oversight, because confident-but-wrong outcomes still occur.",
  "chosen": "b"
 },
 "C3": {
  "context": "At decision point D3, which position does this researcher hold?",
  "a": "Distrusts evaluation outcomes for models that can reason strategically without visible chain-of-thought, since they may identify an evaluation and steer its result.",
  "b": "Prefers evidence of an ulterior motive that is internal or occurs in real or realistic situations over prompted or simulated behavior, because prompted results only test the model+prompt combination.",
  "chosen": "a"
 },
 "C4": {
  "context": "At decision point D3, which position does this researcher hold?",
  "a": "Distrusts surface-level safety testing that only checks whether AIs ever appear to behave badly, since models can appear well-behaved while harboring secret motives.",
  "b": "Prefers a comparison against a stronger model given the same ground-truth data over raw accuracy as evidence, because it rules out the explanation that the answer is derivable from training data.",
  "chosen": "a"
 },
 "C5": {
  "context": "At decision point D6, which position does this researcher hold?",
  "a": "Prefers hedged, nuanced claims that acknowledge normative ambiguity and mixed effects over overclaiming, because some behaviors (deferring when challenged) are not clearly wrong.",
  "b": "Treat single-setting results as modest evidence only; claim the methodology and building blocks transfer rather than the specific numbers or protocol rankings.",
  "chosen": "a"
 }
}
